WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.712
You earn money every month.

00:00:02.732 --> 00:00:05.770
So why does it still feel like
you're starting from zero?

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:09.334
Payday comes, the bills go out,
and by the end of the month,

00:00:09.354 --> 00:00:11.816
the account looks almost
the same as before.

00:00:11.836 --> 00:00:14.835
Today, let's fix that
with a simple system.

00:00:14.855 --> 00:00:17.176
Not a trick. A system.

00:00:17.446 --> 00:00:20.033
By the end of this episode,
you'll know five things.

00:00:20.053 --> 00:00:21.914
Where you actually stand.

00:00:21.934 --> 00:00:24.456
What one hour of your
work is really worth.

00:00:24.476 --> 00:00:26.175
Where your money flows.

00:00:26.195 --> 00:00:27.289
What you're aiming for.

00:00:27.309 --> 00:00:29.670
And how to grow your
income in honest ways.

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:31.988
One quick note about this channel.

00:00:32.128 --> 00:00:35.057
We leave out interest-based
debt and savings, gambling,

00:00:35.077 --> 00:00:36.787
and get-rich-quick schemes.

00:00:36.807 --> 00:00:40.904
We stick to real work,
real activity, and clear numbers.

00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:43.824
Let's follow one example
the whole way through.

00:00:43.844 --> 00:00:44.736
Meet Alex.

00:00:44.756 --> 00:00:46.686
Alex has a regular job.

00:00:46.706 --> 00:00:50.506
Take-home pay, the money that actually
lands in the account after taxes and

00:00:50.526 --> 00:00:53.195
deductions, is $4,000 a month.

00:00:53.215 --> 00:00:56.109
These are teaching numbers,
not average salaries.

00:00:56.129 --> 00:00:58.206
Swap in your own as we go.

00:00:58.476 --> 00:01:01.481
Step one: be honest about
where you stand right now.

00:01:01.501 --> 00:01:03.083
Not where you hope to be.

00:01:03.103 --> 00:01:04.743
Not where your friends seem to be.

00:01:04.763 --> 00:01:05.748
Where you are.

00:01:05.768 --> 00:01:10.260
Alex opens last month's statement
and sorts every expense into groups.

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:13.443
$4,000 comes in. Then it splits.

00:01:13.463 --> 00:01:16.984
Rent, groceries, transportation,
utilities and phone,

00:01:17.004 --> 00:01:18.447
health and insurance.

00:01:18.467 --> 00:01:21.275
These are the essentials: $2,600.

00:01:21.295 --> 00:01:25.206
Then dining out and delivery,
subscriptions, shopping and fun.

00:01:25.277 --> 00:01:26.680
Another $600.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.590
Total spending: $3,200.

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:31.360
So what's left?

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:35.246
$4,000 minus $3,200 equals $800.

00:01:35.266 --> 00:01:38.938
That $800 is not a number to
feel guilty or proud about.

00:01:38.958 --> 00:01:40.412
It's your starting point.

00:01:40.432 --> 00:01:42.208
And now you can see it.

00:01:42.478 --> 00:01:46.249
Step two: find out what one hour
of your work is really worth.

00:01:46.269 --> 00:01:48.873
Alex is paid for about 40 hours a week.

00:01:48.893 --> 00:01:52.588
But work takes more than that:
the commute, getting ready,

00:01:52.608 --> 00:01:53.895
those late emails.

00:01:53.915 --> 00:01:57.791
Add it all up, and Alex gives
about 200 hours a month to work.

00:01:57.811 --> 00:02:02.524
$4,000 divided by 200 hours:
about $20 for each hour.

00:02:02.794 --> 00:02:05.660
This simple math can flip
how you compare jobs.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.594
Job A pays $4,500 a month
but really takes 250 hours.

00:02:10.614 --> 00:02:12.254
That's $18 an hour.

00:02:12.274 --> 00:02:15.784
Job B pays $4,000 for 160 hours.

00:02:15.804 --> 00:02:17.590
That's $25 an hour.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:20.581
The bigger paycheck isn't
always the better hour.

00:02:20.851 --> 00:02:23.269
This is not what Alex
is worth as a person.

00:02:23.289 --> 00:02:25.342
Your value isn't an hourly rate.

00:02:25.362 --> 00:02:27.130
It's a tool for decisions.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:31.677
A $120 pair of sneakers costs
Alex about six hours of work.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.234
Food delivery three times
a week at about $20 each?

00:02:35.254 --> 00:02:37.226
That's roughly three hours, every week.

00:02:37.246 --> 00:02:38.724
Sometimes that's worth it.

00:02:38.744 --> 00:02:40.140
Sometimes it's not.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:41.493
Now the choice is clear.

00:02:41.513 --> 00:02:45.882
Try it yourself: your monthly take-home pay,
divided by all the hours work

00:02:45.902 --> 00:02:47.329
really takes.

00:02:47.599 --> 00:02:50.662
Step three: understand
where income can come from.

00:02:50.682 --> 00:02:52.078
Here's a simple map.

00:02:52.098 --> 00:02:54.674
Think of it as a sketch,
not a law of success.

00:02:54.694 --> 00:02:58.721
One: a job. You trade time
and skill for a paycheck.

00:02:58.741 --> 00:03:01.240
Two: self-employed.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:05.699
You sell your own service,
like design, repairs, or tutoring.

00:03:05.719 --> 00:03:09.089
You keep more control,
but you also handle clients, costs,

00:03:09.109 --> 00:03:10.129
and slow months.

00:03:10.149 --> 00:03:15.156
Three: a business. You build a system
and a team, so the work doesn't depend

00:03:15.176 --> 00:03:16.475
only on your own hours.

00:03:16.495 --> 00:03:19.737
Four: owning productive assets.

00:03:19.757 --> 00:03:23.824
Part of a real business, a property you
rent out, or creative work that people

00:03:23.844 --> 00:03:25.201
buy again and again.

00:03:25.221 --> 00:03:28.250
None of these boxes is better by default.

00:03:28.356 --> 00:03:30.716
A great job can be a strong foundation.

00:03:30.736 --> 00:03:35.611
And every extra source takes time,
money, and real risk to build.

00:03:35.881 --> 00:03:39.460
Now, three words people often
mix up: assets, liabilities,

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:40.500
and cash flow.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:44.018
An asset is something you
own that has value: savings,

00:03:44.038 --> 00:03:46.079
tools, a car, a home.

00:03:46.099 --> 00:03:50.358
A liability is something you owe to someone
else, like an unpaid bill or a debt.

00:03:50.378 --> 00:03:53.813
And cash flow is simply money
moving in or out each month.

00:03:53.833 --> 00:03:56.553
Imagine someone who owns a house outright.

00:03:56.573 --> 00:04:00.883
It's an asset. It still has costs:
property tax, insurance,

00:04:00.903 --> 00:04:04.599
repairs. Those costs are cash going out.

00:04:04.619 --> 00:04:06.730
They don't turn the
house into a liability.

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:10.870
And if they rent out a spare room,
that rent is cash coming in.

00:04:10.941 --> 00:04:12.523
Three separate things.

00:04:12.543 --> 00:04:15.607
Keep them separate,
and the picture gets clear.

00:04:15.877 --> 00:04:17.587
Let's watch how money moves.

00:04:17.607 --> 00:04:19.265
There are three common patterns.

00:04:19.285 --> 00:04:23.816
Pattern one: money comes in, and all
of it goes straight out to expenses.

00:04:23.836 --> 00:04:25.564
Every month resets to zero.

00:04:25.584 --> 00:04:30.220
Pattern two: money comes in, and a big
part goes to payments for things bought

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:31.729
with borrowed money.

00:04:31.749 --> 00:04:34.963
Next month's paycheck is already
spoken for before it arrives.

00:04:34.983 --> 00:04:40.513
Pattern three: money comes in, expenses
are covered, and a planned part stays.

00:04:40.533 --> 00:04:45.575
It builds a reserve, funds goals and skills,
and over time can go into real,

00:04:45.595 --> 00:04:46.870
productive activity.

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:50.151
Same paycheck. Very different direction.

00:04:50.421 --> 00:04:53.113
So where does money slip
away before you even notice?

00:04:53.133 --> 00:04:58.163
First, deductions. Alex's gross
pay is higher than $4,000,

00:04:58.183 --> 00:05:01.182
because taxes and
deductions come out first.

00:05:01.202 --> 00:05:05.210
Paying taxes honestly is part of the deal,
so read your pay stub and know the

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:07.295
difference between gross and take-home.

00:05:07.315 --> 00:05:13.599
Second, charges: late fees, overdraft
fees, and interest on borrowed money.

00:05:13.619 --> 00:05:18.232
On this channel, we avoid interest-based
debt, and simply paying bills on time

00:05:18.252 --> 00:05:19.968
removes a lot of these charges.

00:05:19.988 --> 00:05:23.857
Third, small automatic spending:
subscriptions you forgot,

00:05:23.877 --> 00:05:26.628
delivery fees, impulse buys.

00:05:26.898 --> 00:05:31.162
Here's the rule that holds everything
together: spend less than you earn.

00:05:31.182 --> 00:05:32.225
Clearly less.

00:05:32.245 --> 00:05:35.209
But this isn't about cutting
until life gets gray.

00:05:35.229 --> 00:05:37.078
The essentials stay.

00:05:37.098 --> 00:05:39.674
Rest, family, and small joys stay too.

00:05:39.694 --> 00:05:41.613
Alex just looks for leaks.

00:05:41.633 --> 00:05:44.492
Delivery goes from $300 to $200.

00:05:44.512 --> 00:05:48.852
Two unused subscriptions get
canceled, from $90 to $40.

00:05:48.872 --> 00:05:52.927
Spending drops from $3,200 to $3,050.

00:05:52.947 --> 00:05:58.391
The leftover grows from $800 to $950,
and life didn't get worse.

00:05:58.661 --> 00:06:01.346
Step four: decide what that money is for.

00:06:01.366 --> 00:06:03.384
A goal without a number is just a wish.

00:06:03.404 --> 00:06:07.947
A good goal is specific, measurable,
realistic, meaningful to you,

00:06:07.967 --> 00:06:09.677
and has a deadline.

00:06:09.697 --> 00:06:12.057
"I want to be rich" fails every test.

00:06:12.077 --> 00:06:15.442
"Build a $7,800 reserve
in 13 months" passes.

00:06:15.462 --> 00:06:22.512
Alex splits the $950 on purpose:
$600 to a reserve, $250 to a skills fund,

00:06:22.532 --> 00:06:25.143
and $100 for giving and small joys.

00:06:25.163 --> 00:06:29.845
The reserve is a cushion for surprises,
like a car repair or a few slow months.

00:06:29.865 --> 00:06:32.318
Alex's target: three months of essentials.

00:06:32.338 --> 00:06:33.950
That's $7,800.

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:37.270
At $600 a month, that takes 13 months.

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:42.467
No promised returns, no tricks,
just steady deposits you can watch grow.

00:06:42.737 --> 00:06:46.984
Step five: grow your income,
starting with the value of your hour.

00:06:47.004 --> 00:06:50.897
Ask yourself one question:
doing roughly what I do now,

00:06:50.917 --> 00:06:55.089
or something close to it, how could
one hour of my work be worth more?

00:06:55.109 --> 00:06:56.854
Maybe a certification.

00:06:56.874 --> 00:06:58.827
A stronger skill your team needs.

00:06:58.847 --> 00:07:00.627
Taking on harder problems.

00:07:00.647 --> 00:07:03.408
Or asking for a raise with
clear results in hand.

00:07:03.428 --> 00:07:07.286
Alex puts the skills fund
into a course and, over time,

00:07:07.306 --> 00:07:09.561
aims for a role that pays more.

00:07:09.581 --> 00:07:12.866
It may take a while,
and nothing is guaranteed.

00:07:12.937 --> 00:07:15.675
But a skill is something you always carry.

00:07:15.945 --> 00:07:19.222
Another way to grow is real,
productive activity.

00:07:19.242 --> 00:07:20.680
Let's look at it honestly.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:24.692
Say Alex starts a small weekend service:
tuning bikes for neighbors.

00:07:24.712 --> 00:07:27.327
In one month, revenue is $600.

00:07:27.347 --> 00:07:30.346
Tools, parts, and ads cost $250.

00:07:30.366 --> 00:07:34.265
Profit: $350,
and that's before taxes on it.

00:07:34.285 --> 00:07:37.922
The next month, revenue is
$300 and costs are $200.

00:07:37.942 --> 00:07:39.670
Profit: just $100.

00:07:39.690 --> 00:07:41.168
That's the honest picture.

00:07:41.188 --> 00:07:42.793
Real profit goes up and down.

00:07:42.813 --> 00:07:45.331
There's no promised rate,
and you can lose money too.

00:07:45.351 --> 00:07:49.000
And if you ever put money into
someone else's business as a partner,

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:53.179
a fair deal shares the actual
profit, and the losses too.

00:07:53.199 --> 00:07:56.902
Not a fixed, guaranteed payout
on the money you put in.

00:07:57.172 --> 00:07:59.648
One more idea: scaling.

00:07:59.668 --> 00:08:03.091
An hour of work that serves one
person can only be sold once.

00:08:03.111 --> 00:08:08.292
But a guide, a design, a course,
or a tool you build once can be useful to many

00:08:08.312 --> 00:08:13.192
people. A business can scale too,
like a second location of a coffee shop that

00:08:13.212 --> 00:08:14.197
already works.

00:08:14.217 --> 00:08:19.220
It still takes time, money, and effort
upfront, and it might not work out.

00:08:19.267 --> 00:08:23.608
But it's one way your income stops
depending only on your own hours.

00:08:23.878 --> 00:08:25.304
Let's put it all together.

00:08:25.324 --> 00:08:26.837
Know where you stand.

00:08:26.857 --> 00:08:28.578
Know the price of your hour.

00:08:28.598 --> 00:08:30.215
Watch where money flows.

00:08:30.235 --> 00:08:31.724
Give every dollar a goal.

00:08:31.744 --> 00:08:34.402
And grow your income in honest ways.

00:08:34.952 --> 00:08:36.592
Here's your move for today.

00:08:36.612 --> 00:08:38.201
It takes about twenty minutes.

00:08:38.221 --> 00:08:40.627
Write down your monthly take-home pay.

00:08:40.647 --> 00:08:42.961
List last month's spending in groups.

00:08:42.981 --> 00:08:45.121
Subtract to find your leftover.

00:08:45.141 --> 00:08:48.529
Then divide your pay by the
real hours your work takes.

00:08:48.549 --> 00:08:53.173
Pick one goal, give it a number,
and make the first small transfer.

00:08:53.193 --> 00:08:55.131
Even a small one counts.

00:08:55.401 --> 00:08:57.947
If this helped,
subscribe for the next episode.

00:08:57.967 --> 00:09:00.844
We'll keep building this system,
one clear step at a time.

00:09:00.864 --> 00:09:02.140
See you there.
